{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_105","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: retrieve_permitted.py\n@@\n-    ('Bradley1952', '10.1093/biomet/39.3-4.324'),\n+    ('Glickman1999', '10.1111/1467-9876.00159'),\n@@\n-    ('Huber1967', '10.1214/1900796889'),\n+    ('Huber1964', '10.1214/aoms/1177703732'),\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-Pairwise judgments offer a common interface for comparing systems even when an absolute quality scale is difficult to define. A user can prefer one response to another; a game supplies a winner; a statistical model turns the resulting comparison graph into scores and ranks. The Bradley--Terry construction is a familiar example \\\\citep{Bradley1952}. Human-preference platforms such as Chatbot Arena make these rankings particularly consequential for model comparison \\\\citep{Chiang2024}. Their interpretation nevertheless requires separating several questions: how uncertain is the ordering under resampling, how well does the model describe a population, and how much can the fitted ordering change when selected observations change?\n+Pairwise judgments offer a common interface for comparing systems even when an absolute quality scale is difficult to define. A user can prefer one response to another; a game supplies a winner; a statistical model turns the resulting comparison graph into scores and ranks. The Bradley--Terry construction is a familiar example \\\\citep{Hunter2004}. Human-preference platforms such as Chatbot Arena make these rankings particularly consequential for model comparison \\\\citep{Chiang2024}. Their interpretation nevertheless requires separating several questions: how uncertain is the ordering under resampling, how well does the model describe a population, and how much can the fitted ordering change when selected observations change?\n@@\n-Bootstrap methodology provides a separate route to assessing variability \\\\citep{Efron1979,Efron1986,Davison1997}. Dependence within observations motivates grouped resampling rather than treating every record as independent \\\\citep{Cameron2008}. Our percentile intervals are conditional diagnostics, not a claim of the bootstrap refinements studied in that literature. More generally, a well-defined fitted statistical procedure need not establish a correct population model \\\\citep{Huber1967}. The stress tests below concern the observed fitted rankings, without assuming that the model captures all preference heterogeneity or sports dynamics.\n+Bootstrap methodology provides a separate route to assessing variability \\\\citep{Efron1979,Efron1986,Davison1997}. Dependence within observations motivates grouped resampling rather than treating every record as independent \\\\citep{Cameron2008}. Our percentile intervals are conditional diagnostics, not a claim of the bootstrap refinements studied in that literature. More generally, robust estimation distinguishes an ideal model from departures from it \\\\citep{Huber1964}. The stress tests below concern the observed fitted rankings, without assuming that the model captures all preference heterogeneity or sports dynamics.\n@@\n-The fixed ridge penalty introduces another cross-panel qualification. Because the loss is summed, the same penalty has different relative strength at different sample sizes. Holding it fixed is necessary for this particular comparison to define an unchanged estimator, yet it does not remove regularization as a possible explanation of cross-panel differences. No penalty sweep or alternative tie-model study is available. Results under explicit tie models, a time-varying sports model, different participant filtering, or another ranking estimator could differ.\n+The fixed ridge penalty introduces another cross-panel qualification. Because the loss is summed, the same penalty has different relative strength at different sample sizes. Holding it fixed is necessary for this particular comparison t