{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-Arena and NBA differ in participant universes, graph density, sampling mechanisms, absolute edit counts, tie handling, and nuisance modeling. A common fraction of observed rows is transparent and easy to reproduce, but it is not a common amount of information or a common real-world manipulation effort. In particular, a fractional budget becomes a single game at the smallest sports cost. The resampling units also differ: document-connected groups versus unique games. The study cannot identify whether an observed contrast arises from evaluation design, data volume, graph geometry, score gaps, or other features.\n+Arena and NBA differ in participant universes, graph density, sampling mechanisms, absolute edit counts, ties, and nuisance modeling. A common row fraction is not a common information budget or manipulation effort: the smallest sports intervention changes a single game. Resampling units also differ. These observations cannot separate evaluation design from data volume, graph geometry, or score gaps.\n@@\n-Resampling selected intervention plans answers whether those particular plans retain an advantage under grouped reweighting of the observed panel. It does not measure the uncertainty of selecting a new plan from new data. An adaptive bootstrap that repeats selection would target a different procedure and computational workload; it was not performed. The recorded resample count also makes the percentile tails coarse. Positive Arena intervals remain useful conditional evidence, but should not be promoted to population-wide or simultaneous-confidence statements. The unresolved sports comparisons belong in the main evidence, not as a caveat hidden after an aggregate headline.\n+Resampling fixed plans assesses their advantage under grouped reweighting, not the uncertainty of selecting new interventions from new data. Repeating selection inside a bootstrap would target a different procedure; it was not performed. Coarse percentile tails further limit inference. Positive Arena intervals remain conditional evidence, while unresolved sports comparisons prevent a universal comparative claim.\n@@\n-Full-order disruption weights every participant pair equally. It can reflect changes among close middle-ranked participants without changing a leading position, and it does not quantify practical utility loss. A threshold for changing the top participant, supported-ordering intervals, prompt-block deletion, annotator-level interventions, or a matched human-versus-judge comparison would require different evidence. None is supplied here. Flipping existing outcomes is also not the same capability as submitting new votes to a deployed platform. We infer neither attacker access nor misconduct from feasible counterfactual edits.\n+Full-order disruption weights every participant pair equally and need not change a leading position or quantify utility loss. Top-participant thresholds, supported-ordering intervals, block deletions, annotator-level interventions, and matched human/judge comparisons require evidence absent here. Flipping existing outcomes also differs from submitting new platform votes. Feasible counterfactual edits establish neither attacker access nor misconduct.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH\nPATH=/tmp/paper-tools:\"'$PATH bash .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex > /tmp/paper-compile.log 2>&1; tail -4 /tmp/paper-compile.log'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
