{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_186","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-Pairwise-comparison rankings compress many observations into an apparently precise ordering, but uncertainty about that ordering is different from sensitivity to changing selected observations. We study finite binary-outcome changes in fixed Bradley--Terry rankings using crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences and season-specific NBA games. An influence-guided, exact-refitted search constructs nested edit sets; uniform, degree-stratified, and small-margin interventions provide matched-budget comparisons. On Arena, changing 43, 216, and 432 outcomes disrupts 2.63\\\\%, 8.73\\\\%, and 11.36\\\\% of participant-pair orderings. The corresponding nominal budgets are 0.1\\\\%, 0.5\\\\%, and 1\\\\%. Across the evaluated NBA seasons, the largest budget disrupts 5.29--7.36\\\\% of orderings. Guided interventions have larger point estimates than the controls, but grouped paired-bootstrap intervals are consistently positive only on Arena; NBA evidence is mixed. These are attainable sensitivity witnesses for the observed panels, not optimal corruption thresholds or causal differences between evaluation designs. Fixed-plan uncertainty and missing component ablations limit broader interpretation.\n+Pairwise-comparison rankings compress many observations into an apparently precise ordering, but uncertainty about that ordering is different from sensitivity to changing selected observations. We study finite binary-outcome changes in fixed Bradley--Terry rankings using crowdsourced Chatbot Arena preferences and season-specific NBA games. An influence-guided, exact-refitted search constructs nested edit sets; uniform, degree-stratified, and small-margin interventions provide matched-budget comparisons. On Arena, changing 43, 216, and 432 outcomes disrupts 2.63\\\\%, 8.73\\\\%, and 11.36\\\\% of participant-pair orderings. The corresponding nominal budgets are 0.1\\\\%, 0.5\\\\%, and 1\\\\%. Across the evaluated NBA seasons, the largest budget disrupts 5.29--7.36\\\\% of orderings. Guided budget-averaged point estimates exceed the controls, but grouped paired-bootstrap intervals consistently favor guidance against every control only on Arena. These are attainable sensitivity witnesses for the observed panels, not optimal corruption thresholds or causal differences between evaluation designs. Fixed-plan uncertainty and missing component ablations limit broader interpretation.\n*** Update File: literature/reading_scope.md\n@@\n-Original abstract text was consulted for all cited sources except Zermelo (1929), whose original scanned article was retrieved from Springer and the opening model/likelihood pages inspected. For Koh, Broderick, Chiang, Jiang, and Negahban, original PDFs were also retrieved and abstract/introduction excerpts inspected. For Bubeck, the original abstract and contents were inspected. These are not claims of cover-to-cover reading. Remaining citations are limited to the general subject or method explicitly described in the original abstract text retained through OpenAlex/Crossref or the original arXiv abstract page. No theorem, numerical result, or novelty comparison is attributed to an uninspected section.\n+Original abstract text was consulted for all cited sources except Zermelo (1929), whose original scanned article was retrieved from Springer and the opening model/likelihood pages inspected. For Koh, Broderick, Chiang, Jiang, Negahban, and Maystre, original PDFs were also retrieved and abstract/introduction excerpts inspected. For Bubeck, the original abstract, front matter and contents were inspected. These are not claims of cover-to-cover reading. Remaining citations are limited to the general subject or method explicitly described in the original abstract text retained through OpenAlex/Crossref or the original arXiv abstract page. No theorem, numeri